  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY NIGHT THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 219 PM CDT Wed Oct 7 2026\n\n(Friday - Friday Night)\n\nThe anomalous mid/upper level ridge flexes back northward on Friday, \nwith 850-hPa temperatures also climbing back toward +18C (>95th \npercentile of climatology). High temperatures are therefore expected \nto rebound right back into the mid to upper 80s, with some spots \npotentially hitting the 90 degree mark. Daily record highs at both \nSt. Louis and Columbia may be threatened. Please see the CLIMATE \nsection below for more details. Some minor negating factors though \ninclude winds more out of the south vs. southwest (not as much of a \ndownslope component, especially for St. Louis) and the potential at \nleast for some mid/high level cloud cover. The overall moderation \ntrend with respect to nighttime lows also is expected to continue, \nmainly due to an increase in southerly flow at the surface. Low \ntemperatures Friday night are only expected to fall back into the \nlow to mid 60s (~10-12F above normal).\n\n\n(Saturday - Sunday)\n\nWe continue to watch the track of Tropical Cyclone Isaias and its \npath northward this weekend. The latest track from the National \nHurricane Center brings the center through west-central \nTennessee/western Kentucky. The edge of the 5-day cone is farther to \nthe west however. If the remnants of Isaias are more toward that \nwestern edge, the chances for (beneficial) rain would increase \nsharply across parts of southeast Missouri and southwest Illinois. \nLREF probabilities for at least 1\" of rain are in the 10-15% range, \nthough have been trending upward as the overall spread in both \nthe timing and the track have been on the downswing. We may know a \nlot more about the track and impacts (if any) in our area as there \nis a pretty clear delineation between guidance that more rapidly \nstrengthens Isaias (more toward the east) vs. weaker solutions (more \ntoward the west). Regardless, at least some modest \"cooling\" during \nthe daytime hours would be likely due to an increase in mid/high \nlevel clouds on the outskirts of any remnants of Isaias, as well as \nsome weak to moderate low-level cold air advection from the \nsoutheast. The spread in high temperatures each day is highest where \nrain is more likely to impact the region. Across parts of southeast \nMissouri and southwest Illinois, the inter-quartile range on the NBM \nis closer to 10 degrees, but drops closer to 5 degrees for the \nnorthwestern CWA.\n\n\n(Sunday Night - Next Wednesday)\n\nThere is high confidence that unseasonably warm temperatures will \ncontinue into early next week. In fact, another run at daily records \nis quite possible areawide on Monday afternoon. Monday actually \nprobably has the best potential at low 90s, with GEFS/EPS mean 850-\nhPa temperatures approaching +20C (~99th percentile). Winds also \nshould veer more to the southwest ahead of an approaching cold \nfront, which would promote downsloping. There is of course though \nsome uncertainty with the frontal timing given that this is 5 days \nout, but the most likely window appears to be Monday night at this \npoint in time. Cooler temperatures are expected behind this front, \nthough readings likely will remain above normal for mid October. \nEven the 25th percentile of the NBM is right near normal, so ~75% of \nmembers have at or above normal values.",
